      "label": "Lemma 5",
      "line": 494,
      "proofDependencies": [],
      "section": "construction",
      "statement": "\\label{lem:graph-transform}\nLet $\\nu\\ge2$ and $d_s\\ge1$ be integers, and let $F=(F_c,F_s)$ be a\n$C^{\\nu+1}$ map from a neighborhood of $(0,0)\\in\\R\\times\\R^{d_s}$ into\n$\\R\\times\\R^{d_s}$.  Suppose that, for some\n$L\\in\\R^{d_s\\times d_s}$,\n\\[\nF(0,0)=(0,0),\\qquad\nDF(0,0)=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&L\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\rho(L)<1.\n\\]\nThen, after restricting the neighborhood, there is a $C^\\nu$ function\n$z=\\zeta(u)$ with $\\zeta(0)=D\\zeta(0)=0$ whose graph is locally forward\ninvariant.  The conclusion does not require $L$, or the full derivative\n$DF(0,0)$, to be invertible.",
      "statementDependencies": [],
      "title": "Construct a smooth invariant graph",
      "type": "Lemma",
      "paperMapping": "The full finite-dimensional spectral-radius theorem. Lean assumes C^ν regularity, weaker than the manuscript’s C^(ν+1) hypothesis, and does not assume invertibility.",
      "texLabel": "lem:graph-transform",
      "relatedDeclarations": [
